Apologies for the late summary, many thanks to the respondents:
Greg Rudd
Martin Petder
Alan Davis
Raul Sossa
=A0
Original question, followed by responses:
Hi Managers,
> I sent out an e-mail a coupla weeks back regarding pros and cons of=20
> SANS and peoples experiences, which I summarised and reposted. Now=20
> that I am becoming a bit more SAN savvy, I would like to drill a bit=20
> deeper and more specifically into peoples SAN experiences. I am at a=20
> bit of a gridlock between an HDS system and the HP EVA3000. I am=20
> interested in any problems people have had with EVA systems and any=20
> good/bad experiences with the Managemant Appliance and software on=20
> EVA's e.g levelling operations, snapshot etc. I really like the look=20
> of the virtualisation and the ease of use of the Management software, =

> but have heard a couple of problems.
=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0=A0
.......................................................................=
.....
..........
Martin Petder:
There were a _lot_ of problems in the beginning of EVA, but they were
resolved with EVAv2. EVAv3 is now out and aside=20
from replication service (which is brand new software for EVA, waiting=20
for it's first "service pack" to be available in the end of this year)=20
everything is working correctly. We have multiple installations of=20
EVA5000 and have had no problems since upgrading to EVAv2.
=A0
Now about EVA3000 - it's a midrange product, as is HDS Thunder series.=20
Aside from the virtualization architecture, which is really awesome=20
(would you really use small RAID groups from small number of disks when =

you can use _all_ your disks for all applications? :) the two storages=20
look quite similar. If you would have any funds I would really suggest=20
going for EVA5000 - because of it's switched back-end. You can quite=20
safely do tricks like pulling out whole shelf full of disks without=20
losing your data (and even not losing a lot of perfomance :) and also=20
easily upgrade your system because of that.
=A0
EVA3000 is running the same software than 5000 series, only about a=20
quarter later. It's already running EVA software version 2, which is a=20
good working solution - and will be running version 3 next month. If =
you=20
want to use replication I would suggest waiting for december when the=20
next software upgrade (with bugfixes) arrives.
=A0
Snapshot and snapclone operations are running fine on EVA - with all=20
operating systems (have tried with Solaris, Tru64, Windows and Linux).=20
(Remember you can't do online copy of your data with HDS, that would=20
survive the loss of original data RAIDset). Nowadays EVA's are also=20
bundled with Enterprise Volume Manager, which is an graphical scripting =

front-end for logical and physical cloning and what can be used =
together=20
with your favourite backup software. Also command line management of =
EVA=20
is quite easy and similar to digital's HSG controller series.
=A0
Automatic levelling in EVA is done only when controller is idle (i.e. =
no=20
serious host activity) - and you almost won't notice it aside from your =

data access to get gradually (and _slowly_ :) faster.
=A0
Biggest problem might be seen with Management Appliance (which was also =

very buggy in it's first versions, but is working correctly now) - what =

is a MS Windows server in enterprise environment :)
=A0
Same time Management Appliance is in no way required for the operation=20
of the EVA - if it breaks or crashes you can safely reboot or reinstall =

it whenever you feel the need. Only thing you would loose is your event =

logs (beside what is being kept also in EVA) and maybe a little time of =

inserting installation CDis into the appliance. Actually there is even=20
quite strong rumour going around about development of the stand-alone=20
version of management software for EVA - so you wouldn't even need=20
Management Appliance.
=A0
HDS also has it's set of problems - especially on the thunder series=20
(and somehow I doubt you are choosing between EVA3k and Lightning :) =
For=20
example they do not fully support multiple-level RAID partitions (i.e.=20
0+1, 10, 0+5 and so on) - as some things (for example replication) are
not possible with these RAID levels. Also during replication HDS =
Thunder=20
is not doing write history logging, thus giving you possibility to=20
seriously corrupt your filesystems and database in replicated area :)=20
For simple operations (i.e. for disk serving) HDS is solid and good=20
product - as is EVA.
